## 1. INTRODUCTION
Polymer solar cells (PSCs) are considered as one of the most attractive candidates for portable and wearable power generators due to their advantages of being lightweight, semitransparent, and mechanically flexible. During the past few years, the power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of the PSCs have increased rapidly to over $17-18\%$ with the development of various small-molecule acceptors (SMAs).1−5 These SMAs that contain strongly light-absorbing dye molecules enable much greater light harvesting than the conventional fullerene-based acceptors, leading to dramatic improvements of the short-circuit current densities $\left(J_{s c}s\right)$ and PCEs.2,3,6−8 However, the SMAs, which contain multiple fused rings in their backbone structures, are highly crystalline and lmoencg-htaenrimcalslty bfirliatgiielse,ofl tmhiet nSgMtAh-ebasmeedchPaSnCics.a9l−1p2roCpoerntsiieds raindg that one of the most attractive features of PSCs is to use them as wearable and portable power generators ,13−15 the mechanical properties of SMA-based PSCs should be significantly enhanced without sacrificing any of their high efficiencies.
To this end, recent efforts have been made to improve the mechanical properties of SMA-based PSCs, mostly by polymerizing the SMA building blocks.9,10,16−20 Polymerization of SMAs can enhance the mechanical properties of SMAs by decreasing the crystallinity and bridging different crystalline domains. For example, the use of polymer acceptors $\left(P_{\mathrm{A}}s\right)$ made from an IDIC-based SMA increased the mechanical ductility of the PSCs with a crack onset strain (COS) of $8.6\%$ , whereas the active layers composed of the same SMA mechanically failed at a strain of less than $2\%$ 9 More recently, we designed new polymerized SMAs which share the same building units with the polymer donors for better molecular compatibility, leading to both efficient (PCE $\approx11\%$ ) and mechanically robust $\mathrm{'COS}~\approx~15\%\mathrm{'}$ ) PSCs.10
However, although the mechanical properties of PSCs strongly rely on the molecular weights (MWs) of the consisting polymer s,12,21−24 polymerized SMAs having high MWs (above the critical MW) have not been achieved thus far. Moreover, the PSCs with polymerized SMAs still show a lower PCE than that of the state-of-the art SMA-based PSCs.5,9,10,19
A more simple and straightforward method to modify the mechanical properties of the SMA-based PSCs is to incorporate ductile ternary additives into the donor:acceptor binary blend.25−32 For example, Chen et al. incorporated poly(dimethylsiloxane-co-methyl phenethylsiloxane) polymer additives into the TQ-F:N2000 solar cell system, resulting in improved toughness and ductility of the active layers.26 Nevertheless, the use of electrically insulating polymeric additives resulted in serious degradation of the PCE of the PSCs.26,33 In that, polymeric acceptors (PAs) having high mechanical and electrical properties at the same time can be exciting candidates for modifying the mechanical properties of SMA-based PSCs.9,12,34−36 In particular, naphthalene diimide (NDI)-based $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}s$ typically have excellent electron mobility and mechanical properties, making them suitable as active components in efficient PSCs.12,37−39 In addition, NDI-based $P_{\mathrm{{A}}^{\mathrm{{S}}}}$ having a high MW above the critical MWessentially required to achieve high mechanical toughness and ductility are easily accessible by coupling reactions.12,21,40−43
Here, we report a simple and efficient strategy of using a high-MW poly[ $[N,N^{\prime}$ -bis(2-octyldodecyl)-naphthalene1,4,5,8-bis(dicarboximide)-2,6-diyl]-alt- ${\boldsymbol5},{\boldsymbol5}^{\prime}$ -( $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bithiophene)] (P(NDI2OD-T2)) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive for simultaneously enhancing the PCE and stretchability of the PSCs. The P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additives are designed to have a very high MW (weight-average molecular weight $(M_{\mathrm{w}})=267\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{mol^{-1}})$ , which is above their critical MW $\left(M_{\mathrm{w}}\approx100\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{mol^{-1}},\right.$ ).12,24,40
The addition of high-MW $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additives improved the PCE value from 14.62 to $15.44\%$ in the PSCs consisting of poly[(2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl-3-fluoro)thiophen-2-yl)- benzo $[1,2-b:4,5-b^{\prime}]$ dithiophene))-alt-(5,5-( $^{1^{\prime},3^{\prime}}$ -di-2-thienyl$s^{\prime},7^{\prime}$ -bis(2-ethylhexyl)benzo $[1^{\prime},2^{\prime}{-}c{:}4^{\prime},5^{\prime}{-}c^{\prime}]$ dithiophene-4,8- dione)] (PM6) donor and $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ - $((2Z,2^{\prime}Z)$ -((12,13-bis(2-ethylhexyl)-3,9-diundecyl-12,13-dihydro-[1,2,5]thiadiazolo[3,4-e]- thieno $\left[{2^{\prime\prime},3^{\prime\prime}:4^{\prime},5^{\prime}}\right]$ thieno $\left[2^{\prime},3^{\prime}{:}4,5\right]$ pyrrolo[3,2-g]thieno$[2^{\prime},3^{\prime}:4,5]$ t h i e n o [ 3 , 2 - b ] i n d o l e - 2 , 1 0 - d i y l ) b i s - (methanylylidene))bis(5,6-dichloro-3-oxo-2,3-dihydro-1H-indene-2,1-diylidene))dimalononitrile (Y7) SMA. Importantly, the addition of the high-MW $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ significantly increased the mechanical ductility of the PM6:Y7 PSCs by more than 4-fold in terms of the COS (i.e., $C O S=2\%$ for the pristine PM6:Y7 blend; $\mathrm{COS}=10\%$ with the addition of $20\ \mathrm{wt}\ \%\ P_{\mathrm{A},}$ . As a result, for the first time, we demonstrate a PSC system satisfying a high efficiency $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\mathrm{PCE}\ =\ 15\%\right)}\end{array}$ and mechanical stretchability $\mathrm{^COS}\ =\ 10\%$ ) at the same time. These simultaneous enhancements by $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additives are mainly attributed to the formation of an entangled network and tie molecules between adjacent acceptor domains, providing both electron hopping pathways and stress dissipation routes. Detailed analyses regarding the electrical, structural, and mechanical properties of the PSCs for various amounts of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ were performed to elucidate their roles.
## 2. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## Basic Material Properties
We employed the PM6 donor and Y7 SMA as a representative of a highly efficient PSC system6 and selected the P(NDI2ODT2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ as a ductilizing additive (Figure 1a). The $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive strategy in this work is distinguished from previous additive strategies employing electrically insulating polymers,26,33,44−46 in that the P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ has high electron mobility due to its electroactive conjugated backbones and strong assembly into the intermolecular structures. Among the various types of $P_{\mathrm{A}}s,$ P(NDI2OD-T2) is one of the most extensively used $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ materials producing high-performance PSC devices ,47−51 due to its superior electron mobility $\left(\mu_{\mathrm{e}}\right)$ and suitable energy level offset with efficient benzodithiophene (BDT)-based polymer donors.13,52,53 These features suggest that the P(NDI2OD-T2) would not deteriorate the electrical properties of the PSC blends when they are embedded as an additive into the PM6:Y7 system. Importantly, synthetic methods to produce very high-MW P(NDI2OD-T2) polymers are easily accessible.24,40 Thus, the P(NDI2OD-T2) having a very high MW is targeted to be synthesized in order to maximize the enhancement of mechanical properties of the PM6:Y7 blend by the addition of P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ . It is noted that the $M_{\mathrm{w}}$ $=267~\mathrm{kg~mol^{-1}}$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ in this study is much higher than the critical MW $\left(M_{\mathrm{w}}\approx100\mathrm{kg}\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\right)$ , above which polymers start to fold and entangle.24,40 Our hypothesis is that tie molecules and entangled chains of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive can effectively dissipate the external mechanical stresses on the PM6:Y7 blend; otherwise, the blend without the additive suffers from brittle crack propagation through sharp interfaces between the PM6 and Y7 domains (Figure 1d).
## Photovoltaic Properties
We investigated the photovoltaic performances of PM6:Y7 systems loaded with different amounts of P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additives. The normalized photovoltaic parameters of the systems are plotted as a function of $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ content in Figure 2a. The detailed results are shown in Table 2. The current density−voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves under simulated 1 sun illumination are plotted in Figure 2b. The PCE distribution histograms of the respective devices in Figure 2c demonstrated a near-Gaussian distribution. We also included the photovoltaic performances of the PM6:P(NDI2OD-T2) blend in Figure S1 and Table S1. To investigate the effect of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content on the photovoltaic properties, the ratio of donor to acceptor in the PSC blends was fixed to 1:1. Thus, the percentage of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ in a PSC blend sample refers to the weight percentage of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ in the total acceptor content $\left(\mathrm{Y}7+\right.$ $P_{\mathrm{{A}}})$ of the PSC. For example, a blend containing 30 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ has a $\mathrm{PM}6{:}\mathrm{Y}7{:}P_{\mathrm{A}}$ weight ratio of 1:0.7:0.3. First, the PM6:Y7 blend without $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content showed a maximum PCE of $14.62\%$ . Notably, the PCE of the PSCs increased to $15.44\%$ with incorporation of 10 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ additive, and it remained at $15.01\%$ with the 20 wt $\%\ P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive (Table 2). The PCE slightly decreased to $14.08\%$ at the $30\mathrm{wt}\%$ addition. The $J_{s c}s$ of the PSCs showed a similar trend to their PCEs, i.e., the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}\mathbf{s}$ initially increased with increasing $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content up to 20 wt $\%$ ; however, the open-circuit voltages $\left(V_{\mathrm{oc}}s\right)$ remained almost constant across the range of contents. However, the
PCE value started to decrease rapidly when the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ amounts were higher than 30 wt $\%$ , which was mainly caused by the decreases of fill factor (FF) and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . The external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the solar cells loaded with different amounts of $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ are presented in Figure 2d. The calculated $J_{s c}s$ are matched with the actual $J_{\mathrm{sc}}s$ within a $4\%$ error (Table 2). The EQE responses of the PSCs in the Y7 absorption range $\left(800-950\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ decreased, but the EQE values in the PM6 absorption range $\left(450\mathrm{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}\right)$ increased with increasing $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content $(0-30~\mathrm{wt}~\%)$ ). Also, the EQE values in the absorption range between 650 and $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ increased for the higher $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content.
## Charge Transport and Recombination Properties
To analyze the charge dissociation behaviors of the PSCs at their donor−acceptor interfaces, their photocurrent densities $\left(J_{\mathrm{ph}}s\right)$ were measured under effective voltage $(V_{\mathrm{eff}})$ (Figure S2).61 The exciton dissociation probabilities $\left(P(\mathrm{E},\mathrm{T})s\right)$ were estimated by obtaining the ratios of $J_{s c}s$ ( $\mathrm{\Delta}V_{\mathrm{eff}}=V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ dashed line in Figure S2) relative to the saturated $J_{\mathrm{{ph}}}\boldsymbol{s}$ ( $\dot{V}_{\mathrm{eff}}=4\:\mathrm{V})$ (Table S3).61 The PM6:Y7 blends with 0 to 10 wt $\%\ P_{\mathrm{A}}$ addition displayed a high $P({\mathrm{E}},{\mathrm{T}})$ of over $90\%$ , but a steep decrease of the $P({\mathrm{E}},{\mathrm{T}})$ values was observed for a high $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ loading of more than $20{-}30$ wt $\%$ (Figure S2). For example, the $P({\mathrm{E}},{\mathrm{\bar{T}}})$ values decreased from $94.4\%$ $(0\mathrm{wt}\%P_{\mathrm{A}})$ to $87.7\%$ $(30\mathrm{wt}\%P_{\mathrm{A}})$ and to $77.3\%$ (50 wt $\%P_{\mathrm{A},}$ ). This result implies that the addition of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ could reduce the donor−acceptor interfaces where exciton dissociation occurs.62
In addition, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the PSCs were measured under different light intensities $(P)$ from 10 to $100\mathrm{\mW\cm}^{-2}$ , to elucidate the charge recombination characteristics (Figure 3). $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ is known to follow an equation of ${\cal J}_{\mathrm{sc}}\propto{\cal P}^{\alpha};$ , and the slope $(\alpha)$ of the power-law relationship becomes closer to unity when bimolecular recombination is suppressed.63 In Figure 3a, the $\alpha$ values in all of the blends were almost the same, around $\alpha=$ 0.94, indicating all the blends did not suffer from severe bimolecular recombinations in their PSC operations. Under the open-circuit condition, $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is proportional to $\ln(P)$ with a unit of $k T q^{-1}$ $k=$ Boltzmann constant, $T=$ temperature, and $q$ $\c=$ elementary charge), and the slope (S) of the linear regression approaches unity when all charge carriers reach to electrodes without monomolecular or trap-assisted recombination.64 A clear difference in the S values of the PSC blends depending on the contents of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ was observed (Figure 3b). The PM6:Y7 blend showed an S value of $1.13\ k\bar{T}q^{-1}$ ; however, those associated with the PSCs containing 10 and 20 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ were lower, i.e., 1.06 and $1.09k T q^{-\tilde{1}}$ , respectively. Thereby, the monomolecular or trap-assisted recombinations became suppressed by an appropriate amount of $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive. However, the S values increased to 1.23 and $1.33\:k T q^{-1}$ for the 40 and 50 wt $\%P_{\mathrm{A}}$ loadings, respectively. It suggests that excessive amounts of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ cause charge recombination. As a result, the increases of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE with the 10 and 20 wt $\%P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additions compared to the blend without additive can be explained by the suppressed monomolecular/trap-assisted recombinations.
## Mechanical Properties of the Thin Films
To demonstrate the importance of $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive in enhancing the mechanical properties of the PM6:Y7 blend film, the tensile properties of both pristine and $\mathrm{PM}6{:}\mathrm{Y}7{:}P_{\mathrm{A}}$ blend thin films were investigated using a pseudo-free-standing tensile test.65−67 Each thin film was transferred from its glass substrate onto a water surface to measure its intrinsic tensile properties in a pseudo-free-standing state. Figure S3 and Table S4 show the stress−strain (S−S) curves, COSs, calculated toughnesses, and elastic moduli $\left(E s\right)$ of the pristine constituent materials. While the PM6 polymer exhibited a moderate ductility (COS $=14.66\%$ ) and toughness $\left(3.84\mathrm{MJ}\mathrm{m}^{-3}\right)$ , the tensile properties of Y7 could not be measured due to extreme brittleness of the thin films. This brittleness of the Y7 thin film was attributed to its highly rigid ladder-type backbone structure, which is consistent with the reported characteristic brittleness of SMA materials.10,12 In stark contrast, the P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ had high ductility with a COS and toughness of $39.20\%$ and 12.0 MJ $\mathbf{m}^{-3}$ , respectively. The superior ductility of P(NDI2ODT2) was attributed to its high MW $\left(M_{\mathrm{w}}\:=\:267\:\mathrm{\kg\mol^{-1}}\right)$ exceeding its critical MW, which affords the formation of tie molecules and entangled chains in the thin film.12,24,40
Figure 4a displays the S−S curves of the blend thin films, and Table S5 lists their COS, toughness, and $E$ values. Without the P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content, the PM6:Y7 binary blend showed brittle tensile properties, showing only $2.21\%$ of COS and $0.31\mathrm{MJ}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ of toughness. This was ascribed to the failure of stress dissipations due to the brittleness of the Y7 SMA.10,12,68 The addition of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ significantly increased the COS and toughness values of the blends. For example, the addition of 30 and $50\mathrm{wt}\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ increased the COS values to 11.93 and $19.97\%$ , respectively, and the toughness values to 3.76 and $5.15\mathrm{~MJ~m~}^{-3}$ , respectively (Figure 4b). The crack formation behavior of blend thin films with and without $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ contents (see the images in Figure 4c) was distinctly different. The pristine PM6:Y7 thin film developed a brittle break (upper image), whereas the blend thin film containing the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ experienced ductile plastic deformation, resulting in significant wrinkling (lower image). This notable enhancement in the mechanical ductility of the blends was attributed to tie molecules and chain entanglements formulated by the long chains of high-MW $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}{},$ dramatically improving the dissipation of external stress.10,68 Based on the aforementioned results and the photovoltaic parameters presented in Figure 2 and Table 2, the PSC incorporating 20 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ demonstrates optimal photovoltaic performance (PCE exceeding $15\%$ ) and mechanical robustness (COS exceeding $10\%$ ). The PCEs and COS values (obtained by the pseudo-free-standing tensile test) of the PSCs developed in this study were compared with those of previously reported PSCs (Figure 4d and Table S6).
## Morphological Properties
To better understand the variances in the photovoltaic/ mechanical properties of the PSCs with different $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents, their blend morphologies including the domain sizes and purities were investigated using resonant soft X-ray scattering (RSoXS) (Figure 5).69 The scatterings were acquired in the incidence beam energy of $284.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ which can maximize the material contrasts in the blends.69 The RSoXS profiles of all the blends reflect clear peaks at $q~=~0.0081{\mathrm{\boldmath~\hat{A}^{-1}~}}$ (domain spacing $\mathbf{\Sigma}=~77~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ). Notably, the scattering peaks got more distinct and sharper with increasing $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content, suggesting gradational development of independent phases of the blend components.70 For a more quantitative analysis, we estimated the relative domain purity of each blend, which is proportional to the square root of the integrated scattering intensity $(\sqrt{\mathrm{ISI}})$ (Table S7).71 To note, the addition of 10 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ increased the relative domain purity to 0.20. This suggests that even small amounts of the high-MW $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ can formulate its own crystalline domains, owing to the highly semicrystalline and self-aggregating properties of the long $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ chains.40 As the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content of the blend increased, the relative domain purities gradually increased. For example, the addition of 20 and 40 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ increased the relative domain purity of the blend to 0.49 and 1.00, respectively. These results indicate that high $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content promoted the formation of domains with high purities.
The structural properties of the pristine constituent materials and the blend films were estimated using grazing incidence $\mathrm{x}$ -ray scattering (GIXS) (Figures S4 and S5 and 6). The GIXS line-cut profiles of both the pristine and blend films from GIXS are represented in Figures S4 and 6, respectively. In Figure S4, all of the PM6, Y7, and P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ showed the face-on dominant packing orientations with in-plane (IP)
(100) peaks and out-of-plane (OOP) (010) peaks, which is beneficial to the vertical charge transport properties.72,73 Especially, both Y7 and $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ showed distinct (001) scatterings in the $\mathrm{IP}$ direction with $d$ spacings of 15.9 and $^{13.8\ \check{\mathrm{A}},}$ respectively.74 The scattering profiles in Figure 6 indicate that the blend films also had the face-on dominant packing orientations, consistent with those of the pristine constituent materials. Interestingly, the IP (001) peaks of Y7 and $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ showed different patterns depending on the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents. The PM6:Y7 blend without the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ did not show any peak around $q$ $=0.40{-}0.46\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1}$ in the IP direction, while the blends with 10 and 20 wt $\%\ P_{\mathrm{A}}$ showed detectable peaks in the same regime (Figure 6a). Meanwhile, in the profiles of the blends containing 40 and 50 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}{},$ two separate peaks were observed. In a quantitative analysis of the GIXS results, the $d$ spacing and coherence length $\left(\boldsymbol{L}_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ values of the blends associated with the IP (001) peaks were calculated (Table S8). The $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ values were estimated by a Scherrer equation,75 and the procedure is detailed in the Experimental Section. It is noted that absolute value of $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ would be varied from the actual size of crystals, since the solution-processed blends consisting of semicrystalline polymers and small molecules can be fairly disordered. The $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ values of the (001) scattering of P(NDI2OD-T2) $\left(q_{x y}=0.46\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\right)$ increased significantly with increasing $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$ content, e.g., from 12.9 to $16.4\ \mathrm{nm}$ as the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content increased from 30 to 50 wt $\%$ . The $L_{\mathrm{c}}$ values associated with the (001) scattering of Y7 $\left(q_{x y}=0.40\mathrm{\AA}^{-1}\right)$ also increased with increasing $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content, e.g., from 10.7 to $14.8~\mathrm{nm}$ as the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content increased from 30 to 50 wt $\%$ . In addition, distinct (200) peaks in the OOP direction associated with $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ - (NDI2OD-T2) $\left(q_{z}=0.58\ \mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}\right)$ were observed in the profiles of the blends containing 40 and 50 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}{},$ while the profiles of the blends containing less than 40 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ did not show any peaks. The $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ forms its own crystal networks in the blends even at low $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents $(10-20\mathrm{~wt~}\%)$ , and independent crystalline domains of Y7 and $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ are evident in blends with high $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents $(40-50~\mathrm{wt}~\%)$ . These results are consistent with the observations from the RSoXS analyses.
From the electrical, mechanical, and morphological analyses of the blends, the different blend morphologies are described in Figure 7 in terms of $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content. The PM6:Y7 blend (without $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content) includes locally isolated Y7 domains (Regime I). In this regime, some of the electrons become trapped in isolated Y7 domains. Moreover, mechanical stresses concentrate along sharp and fragile PM6−Y7 interfaces, avoiding the hard Y7 crystallites; thereby, cracks propagate easily under small strains (i.e., a COS of $2\%$ ).12,76,77 In blends with optimal $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents $\left(10{-}20~\mathrm{wt}~\%\right)$ , the three components formulate properly intermixed domains with moderate domain purities (Regime II). Tie molecules formed by long $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ chains bridge and interconnect the isolated Y7 domains. This facilitates the effective hopping of electrons from one acceptor domain to the adjacent ones, suppressing monomolecular or trap-assisted recombinations in comparison to the blend without the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ . Also, the entangled chains and tie molecules of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ effectively dissipate external mechanical stress, making the blend films more resistant to the crack formation and mechanical failure. In comparison, the blends with excessive $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ contents (over 30 wt $\%$ ) have highly segregated and pure domains of the three components with comparatively smaller donor−acceptor interfacial areas (Regime III). In this regime, the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ does not percolate well to the PM6 and Y7 domains, owing to their high purities and severe segregation. In conclusion, the blends containing 10−20 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ exhibit optimal photovoltaic and mechanical properties, demonstrating $15\%$ of PCE and $10\%$ of COS simultaneously.
## 3. CONCLUSIONS
We developed a PSC system that demonstrates both a high PCE and mechanical robustness, by incorporating a high-MW $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ additive into the SMA-based PSC blend. This additive approach is highly effective in enhancing the mechanical properties of the characteristically brittle SMA-based PSCs without compromising any photovoltaic performances. The addition of an appropriate amount of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ ( $\leq20$ wt $\%$ ) increased the PCE of the PM6:Y7 PSC, while excessive amounts of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ decreased its PCE. For example, the PSCs incorporating 10 and 20 wt $\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ demonstrated higher PCEs (15.44 and $15.01\%$ , respectively) than the PSC featuring the pristine blend $\mathrm{'PCE}=14.62\%$ . We revealed that the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content of a blend determines the extent of its acceptor-phase separation and, consequently, its electrical and photovoltaic performances in the PSC by affecting its charge transport and recombination behaviors. Importantly, the mechanical ductility of the blend film dramatically increased with increasing $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content, e.g., its COS increased from 2 to over $10\%$ as the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ content increases from 0 to 20 wt $\%$ . Based on these results, the blend containing $20\%$ of the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ produced optimal PSC devices that simultaneously demonstrate high photovoltaic performance $\mathrm{'PCE}>15\%$ ) and mechanical robustness (COS $>10\%$ ). Thus, we developed an effective strategy for achieving PSCs with high PCEs potentially suitable for stretchable or wearable applications.
## 4. EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
## Polymerization of P(NDI2OD-T2) $P_{\mathrm{{A}}}$
The $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ was prepared by a Stille coupling reaction by following a method from the previous literature.40 Particularly, we performed the polymerization during a long time (i.e., 2 days) to provide sufficient time for the $P_{\mathrm{A}}$ growth and at high temperature $(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ to prevent gelation (yield: $80\%$ , $M_{\mathrm{w}}=267\mathrm{\:\bar{kg}\ m o l^{\bar{-}1}}$ , $\varTheta=2.3$ ).
## Materials
The 2,9-bis(3-((3-(dimethylamino)propyl)amino)propyl)anthra$\left[2,1,9-d e f;6,5,10-d^{\prime}e^{\prime}f^{\prime}\right]$ diisoquinoline- $\bar{1},\bar{3},8,10\big(2\bar{H},9\bar{H}\big)$ -tetraone (PDINN) material was synthesized by following a method described in the literature.78 PM6 $P_{\mathrm{D}}$ and Y7 SMA were purchased from Derthon. PEDOT:PSS (AI4083) was purchased from Heraeus. The other chemical compounds were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
